WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.590
The Archivist: “When you’re designing
a speculative creature, where do you like

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.819
to start?”

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:13.720
Alex Ries: “I’ll tend to gather, maybe
10 or 20 references that are in the theme

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.390
of the direction I’m going.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:22.529
Reference images of fossil life, reconstructions,
living things, even down to fungi and microscopic

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:23.529
creatures.

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:27.259
And then I bring that up in photoshop and
start sketching — referencing that.”

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:33.550
That’s Alex Ries, a creature designer for
Subnautica: Below Zero, and one of the most

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:36.949
talented speculative biology artists out there.

00:00:36.949 --> 00:00:42.770
Many of the creatures that swim in the seas
of Planet 4546 B started off as his idea,

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:48.590
going through multiple iterations of concept
art to arrive at the awe-inspiring final designs

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:49.879
we see in game.

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:55.170
Yet there’s a lot of incredible art of unique
speculative lifeforms that — for one reason

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.429
or another — never made it into the final
product.

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:03.710
Not implementing all designs from early development
stages is a normal part of pre-production,

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.770
but some of these designs are so well thought-out,
they deserve to be studied further.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:15.500
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
explore the incredible unseen creatures of

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.720
Subnautica, exploring their potential biology
and how they might fit into the existing environments

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.610
of Planet 4546B.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:29.500
And if you like the art showcased in this
video, you can support Alex Ries on Patreon

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.000
using the links below.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.270
Now, let’s return to the water of Planet
4546B, and explore the speculative lifeforms

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.460
that could have been…

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:47.240
Beginning in the vast, open regions of Planet
4546B’s ocean, some very unique leviathans

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.810
almost swam.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:53.960
These enormous, curiously shaped creatures
are called Thalassaceans — and are imagined

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.210
to fill the niche of a peaceful filter feeder,
straining small food particles from the water

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.780
using specialized mouths.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.870
Their fin structure gives them a distinctive
profile, and Alex Ries says the creature uses

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:12.390
its rear fins to swim in a manner similar
to the oceanic sunfish of Earth.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.330
Ries created a lot of different color variants
exploring the options for pigment and skin

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:22.450
texture for these lifeforms, and I have to
say, some of them are really striking.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:27.070
Like most of the lifeforms on this list, it’s
hard not to wish we could interact with them

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:28.980
in game.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.780
Other potential organisms are more deadly.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.580
One lifeform that would work beneath sandy
environments is the Sand Spear.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:41.530
This terrifying creature is an ambush predator
that buries itself in sediment and waits for

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.730
the unwary to swim overhead before striking.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.380
In behavior and appearance, the Sand Spear
somewhat resembles Earth’s Bobbit Worm:

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.460
an alarming-looking predator that also ambushes
its prey.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:59.290
The Sand Spear’s name and overall lifestyle
also recalls the Sand Shark from the tropics,

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:04.360
another species that buries itself beneath
the sea floor only to emerge in spectacular

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.160
fashion to snag its lunch.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.090
And like the Sand Shark, the Sand Spear attacks
at high speed, with a spikey maw extending

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.200
out to snap up its prey.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.350
In addition to this veritable forest of teeth,
the head of the Sand Spear features two tentacles

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:22.400
that help them sense their targets while buried.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.790
These feelers are similar to the navigational
appendages on the front of certain genuses

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.570
of marine slugs.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.010
All in all, while sand spears would have been
an exciting inclusion, they’d make swimming

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:36.569
near the sea floor much more stressful…

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:42.080
Upon the ice floats, we almost got a very
unusual type of symbiotic organism.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.560
The Gardener Eel is a bizarre creature that
stores a small, tree-like plant in its gullet.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.770
While on the surface, it extrudes the plant
into the waiting sun for photosynthesis.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:57.520
This type of symbiotic relationship between
a plant and animal does appear in the natural

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.570
world, and can be seen in the Earth ‘Leaf
Sheep’ — a fascinating type of sea slug

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.220
I’ve mentioned before in other entries.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.910
Leaf Sheep can use the green algae on their
backs to perform photosynthesis and produce

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.990
simple sugars for food.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:16.400
The Gardener Eel doesn’t spend all day in
the sun, however, and can ‘swallow’ the

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.090
membrane and swim like a more traditional
eel when in danger.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:23.539
A really inventive lifeform.

00:04:23.539 --> 00:04:28.249
Just under the ice of Sector Zero, a combative
predator could have been prowling.

00:04:28.249 --> 00:04:34.460
This pugnacious creature is a Bulldog Trapjaw,
a mid-sized creature that doesn’t look particularly

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:37.099
threatening while in a neutral position.

00:04:37.099 --> 00:04:42.139
Swim too close, however, and the Trapjaw lives
up to its name by lashing out with a pair

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:44.759
of long, raptorial jaws.

00:04:44.759 --> 00:04:49.900
These extendible mandibles have multiple functions
— useful both as an offensive tool and a

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.460
grasping mechanism to pull prey into their
throat.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.590
The various species of moray eels in Earth
oceans have a convergent adaptation.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:03.479
Their secondary set of raptorial jaws are
located farther back in the eels’ heads,

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:06.270
and contain their own set of sharp teeth.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:11.960
Like the Trapjaw, moray eels use these secondary
jaws to restrain prey and drag them deeper

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.460
into their gullet.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:18.240
Ultimately, the Trapjaw — like the moray
— is a creature you’d have to be wary

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.979
of on a dive.

00:05:20.979 --> 00:05:25.240
Deeper into the depths, another dangerous
lifeform could have been waiting in the eerie

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.479
light of the Crystal Caves.

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:33.479
The Shardrunner is a patient hunter, spinning
out long, fragile mineralized tripwires to

00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:34.900
detect prey.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:40.760
When an unlucky soul touches a wire, the Shardrunner
knows their location and strikes with lethal

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:41.879
accuracy.

00:05:41.879 --> 00:05:46.639
This kind of behavior is almost convergent
with that of certain spiders on land, which

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:52.150
likewise can sense any prey that becomes entangled
in the filaments of their web.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:57.300
Some spiders can sense their prey using macroreceptory
organs called silt sensilla.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:03.509
So, it’s likely the Shardrunner has developed
an analogous sensory organ to detect lifeforms

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:07.360
entangled in its own mineralized web.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:13.139
Notably, some of these hypothetical lifeforms
are relatives of preexisting creatures that

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:18.990
evolved to fill different niches, and as a
result possess highly divergent biology.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:25.110
One of the most awe-inspiring creatures from
Planet 4546B is the Reefback Leviathan of

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.680
the tropics.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:32.330
These gentle giants almost had a polar relative
in the form of the Icebreaker Leviathan, a

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:34.689
concept which is one of Alex Ries’s favorites.

00:06:34.689 --> 00:06:38.623
The Archivist: “So, if you could add one
creature you created that wasn’t in the

00:06:38.623 --> 00:06:41.240
final game into Subnautica: Below Zero, what
would it be?

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.460
Alex Ries: “I would have gone probably with
the Icebreaker Leviathan — Remembering the

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:47.909
Reefbacks and the impact they had on me.

00:06:47.909 --> 00:06:52.819
I thought bringing that creature, that design
into the Arctic environment could be interesting.

00:06:52.819 --> 00:06:56.470
And one possible take could be that they have
to forge their way through the ice.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:00.909
I knew that would have a lot of implications
when you have a creature interacting the environment

00:07:00.909 --> 00:07:01.909
like that.

00:07:01.909 --> 00:07:04.919
It could be a sort of a nesting place for
the Pengwings to live on.

00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:08.370
It could be sort of a moving ice-flow almost.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.539
In the end it wasn’t included, I think,
it just would have been very, very difficult

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:13.680
to implement in terms of programing.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.520
But I think that would have been my choice.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.410
Aside from the Reefback, out of all the creatures
in the original Subnautica, one of the most

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:26.059
iconic — and most imposing — was the dreaded
Reaper Leviathan.

00:07:26.059 --> 00:07:31.869
For Below Zero, Alex Ries imagined that a
smaller yet more agile relative known as the

00:07:31.869 --> 00:07:35.069
Crested Reaper might terrorize the frigid
waters.

00:07:35.069 --> 00:07:40.219
Roughly the size of an orca, the Crested Reaper
are a very different kind of predator than

00:07:40.219 --> 00:07:42.240
the Reapers we’re familiar with.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.849
In another parallel to Earth orcas, the Crested
Reaper is exceptionally cunning, able to bump

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:52.900
ice flows to knock out of reach prey into
the water — a behavior that real orcas have

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:54.360
been observed doing.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:59.629
The Crested Reaper takes this hunting strategy
a step further, however — with its characteristic

00:07:59.629 --> 00:08:05.020
crest actually a specialized ice-ram that
aids in jostling ice floats.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:10.779
The concept of a smarter, more maneuverable
offshoot of a Reaper Leviathan is really intriguing

00:08:10.779 --> 00:08:14.800
— albeit slightly scary…

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.830
Other imagined offshoots are far less menacing.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:23.660
As an example, we have the Grabsquid: A smaller,
more intelligent polar relative of the huge

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.039
and terrifying Crabsquid.

00:08:26.039 --> 00:08:31.990
Much like how the Crabsquid possess a mysterious
bio-electric discharge, the tiny Grabsquid

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.169
can generate a weak bio-electric field.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:40.740
As a result, the Grabsquid is naturally drawn
to small electrical devices like batteries

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:47.209
— a behavior that mirrors how its deep-water
relative is drawn to larger energy sources.

00:08:47.209 --> 00:08:52.180
Large animals evolving a reduced body size
in response to a limited environment is a

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.050
well-documented form of adaption.

00:08:55.050 --> 00:09:00.370
My favorite Earth example of this are the
channel island mammoths, an isolated population

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:05.550
of mammoths that were almost ten feet, or
three meters shorter, than their mainland

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:06.550
relatives.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:12.260
So, the limited resources of the frigid Sector
Zero shaping the massive Crabsquid into the

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:16.589
tiny Grabsquid holds up from an evolutionary
perspective.

00:09:16.589 --> 00:09:22.089
Curiously, an alternate name for the Grabsquid
offshoot is the Seamonkey, as this concept

00:09:22.089 --> 00:09:27.880
was actually an early take on the quite different-looking
Seamonkeys that made it into the final game.

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:32.230
Some The Archivist: “So, in your opinion,
which creature changed the most during development.”

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:36.250
Alex Ries: “I think actually, the Seamonkey
might have gone through the most iterations,

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:39.839
cause that thing started off a complete blank
slate.

00:09:39.839 --> 00:09:43.880
It took quite a while to arrive on a more
friendly sort of look.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.220
Cause the focus was more on alien-ness.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:49.070
But we tried to make it more mischievous and
readable.

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:53.500
It went in so many strange directions that
one of the creatures spun off into the Trivalve.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:57.140
So, the Trivalve was one of the early Seamonkey
attempts.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:04.000
But it was different enough that we managed
to spin it off into its whole other creature.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.110
In the vast skies above the teeming oceans,
we almost had a unique predator in the form

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:13.730
of the Kitewing, a relative of the much smaller
Skyrays.

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:20.060
The gentle Skyrays can be seen flying over
various regions of Planet 4546B, and are the

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:23.500
only lifeform in the games to evolve powered
flight.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:29.529
The Kitewing would add some exciting variety
to the skies of Subnautica by being not just

00:10:29.529 --> 00:10:31.630
a passive lifeform, but a serious threat.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:32.699
This potent predator uses its large talons
to seize prey and carry them back to its nest.

00:10:32.699 --> 00:10:37.870
Alex Ries imagined a few different variants
of this tyrant of the skies, including one

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:41.160
with extra talons and an imposing crest.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.660
This head crest resembles the crown structures
of certain Pterosaurs, which paleontologists

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:50.880
believe may have emerged for social displays
and improved aerodynamics.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:56.100
So, it’s likely the mighty crest of the
Kitewing serves a similar purpose.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:04.529
It’s easy to imagine these swooping terrors
flying through the skies of planet 4546B…

00:11:04.529 --> 00:11:09.899
Returning to entirely original concepts, in
the twilight of the abyss, a predator unlike

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:12.300
any other could made an appearance.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:17.260
The Ascender Worm might appear like your average,
squid-like organism at a glance.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:21.740
Yet what sets this lifeform apart is its highly
unusual hunting method.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:26.709
The small Ascender Worm might not have the
jaws or muscle to take down large prey — but

00:11:26.709 --> 00:11:30.370
it has another weapon: decompression sickness.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:35.720
When the Ascender Worm hunts, it grabs its
onto unsuspecting prey with two deceptively

00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:41.209
strong tentacles, and rapidly inflates with
gas — dragging its target to the surface

00:11:41.209 --> 00:11:44.740
in the hopes the pressure change will kill
them.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:49.819
Decompression sickness, also called the bends,
is a real danger that divers face, and is

00:11:49.819 --> 00:11:54.220
caused by gaseous bubbles forming inside the
body due to depressurization.

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:58.490
A grim fate indeed.

00:11:58.490 --> 00:12:03.750
Other theoretical lifeforms could have even
stranger methods of attack and defense.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:09.110
The Shard is a creature that travels slowly
through the water vertically like a knife,

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:12.230
luring prey from below with its glowing lure.

00:12:12.230 --> 00:12:16.720
While this glowing lure recalls to mind the
classic angler fish, the Shard’s vertical

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:23.060
swimming posture actually reminds me of another
deep-sea creature — the elusive oarfish,

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:26.180
which has been recorded swimming in a similar
manner.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:30.710
But what really makes the Shard stands out
is its defensive capabilities.

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:37.129
When attacked, it quickly excludes hypersaline
slime which rapidly forms an ice ‘shell’

00:12:37.129 --> 00:12:40.810
around its body — from which it suddenly
swims free.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:45.660
This leaves the predator to attack the shard’s
false ice ‘shadow while the real lifeform

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:46.660
escapes.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:51.800
A surreal, yet ingenious form of defense.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:58.199
One of the more distinctive regions of Subnautica:
Below Zero is the Thermal Spires, an ecosystem

00:12:58.199 --> 00:13:01.810
defined by chimney-shaped hydrothermal vents.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:06.269
One species which could have been part of
this environment is the Ventspider — an

00:13:06.269 --> 00:13:11.790
arthropod-like animal would crawl between
the vents on long, spindly legs.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:17.240
On earth, all kinds of sea spiders have emerged,
with some large bottom dwellers that creep

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:23.709
along the sea floor, and others light enough
to swim… although not particularly gracefully.

00:13:23.709 --> 00:13:28.029
Yet the Ventspider is unique due to its method
of getting food.

00:13:28.029 --> 00:13:33.610
It possesses feathery gills which gather sulfur
from around the vents to feed extremophile

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:36.199
bacteria in its abdomen.

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:41.190
Strange as this may sound, hydrothermal vents
on earth are home to all manner of specialized

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:45.930
lifeforms that feed off the rich chemical
soup they produce.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:50.860
Among these lifeforms are bacteria, which
have evolved to be highly resistant to the

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:53.069
heat of the boiling vents.

00:13:53.069 --> 00:13:58.769
So, the idea of a symbiotic alliance between
such bacteria and a larger lifeform like a

00:13:58.769 --> 00:14:03.490
Ventspider isn’t so far-fetched after all...

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:07.980
In the original games, there’s all sorts
of dangerous creatures to look out for.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:13.689
But one particularly unnerving concept would
make these lifeforms even more deadly.

00:14:13.689 --> 00:14:19.790
The Rotnest is a parasite which would infect
other creatures on Planet 4546 B.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:24.740
This parasite would take over the brains of
their hosts, and transform into a launching

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:30.570
platform for jet-propelled spore pods, which
would explode and infect nearby lifeforms

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.990
— spreading the infection.

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:37.300
Terrifying as this concept sounds, we can
find something similar on Earth.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:42.660
One infamous type of fungus can take over
the bodies of ants, hijacking their mind and

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:48.930
essentially turning them into zombies — using
them to move to an ideal spot to sprout and

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.570
spread their spores to other lifeforms.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:57.720
And the Rotnest works the same way, with their
host eventually passing away and the Rotnest

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:03.339
slowly digesting what remains — and launching
spore pods at any creatures foolish enough

00:15:03.339 --> 00:15:04.339
to investigate.

00:15:04.339 --> 00:15:07.699
A truly menacing concept.

00:15:07.699 --> 00:15:12.810
Yet out of all the creatures in Subnautica:
Below Zero, the Shadow Leviathan is perhaps

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:14.879
the most intimating and well-known.

00:15:14.879 --> 00:15:19.839
At multiple points throughout its development,
however, it looked quite different.

00:15:19.839 --> 00:15:23.579
The Archivist: “I’m curious, what was
your process like for designing the Shadow

00:15:23.579 --> 00:15:24.579
Leviathan?

00:15:24.579 --> 00:15:25.579
How did you settle on the final look for that
creature?”

00:15:25.579 --> 00:15:26.999
Alex Ries: “It came to a fork in the road
with that creature.

00:15:26.999 --> 00:15:29.160
We needed something big and scary.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:34.329
I did a bunch of initial sketches which had
a more tradition al— almost like a dragon-like

00:15:34.329 --> 00:15:35.329
head.

00:15:35.329 --> 00:15:40.440
It had glowing white teeth and huge jaws like
a deep-sea viperfish and stuff like that,

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.820
but sort of vertebrate looking, almost.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:48.480
But, I also had a bunch of references of things
called arrow worms.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.939
Horrifying little invertebrates that have
a face that’s basically a bunch of nested

00:15:52.939 --> 00:15:54.459
spines coming out of it.

00:15:54.459 --> 00:15:59.519
It just did one little study where instead
of the dragon-like face, it was this more

00:15:59.519 --> 00:16:00.970
invertebrate face.

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:04.279
Among all those that sort of connected with
people a little bit more.

00:16:04.279 --> 00:16:06.019
It was sort of disturbing.

00:16:06.019 --> 00:16:10.550
And combining that with a bit of lamprey,
and a few other marine creatures that people

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:14.000
generally find horrifying, we moved in that
direction.”

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:18.129
The Archivist: “It definitely scared me
during my playthrough.”

00:16:18.129 --> 00:16:22.070
And there’s one leviathan which didn’t
make it into the final game which might have

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:24.949
been even larger than the Shadow Leviathan.

00:16:24.949 --> 00:16:29.639
The Ice Dragon is a cut creature with such
a size and design so intimidating that it

00:16:29.639 --> 00:16:32.199
lives up to its mythical namesake.

00:16:32.199 --> 00:16:37.589
We can see from the concept art that the Ice
Dragon would swim on wing like flippers — another

00:16:37.589 --> 00:16:40.400
visual nod to the dragons of Earth myth.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:45.699
This alien dragon wouldn’t breathe fire,
however, but release a stream of brinicle

00:16:45.699 --> 00:16:50.880
ice breath to trap vehicles — sort of like
a scaled-up version of the brine fish in the

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:54.110
final game, which Alex Ries also designed.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:59.500
In fact, the Ice Dragon is so large, its size
could have been an issue in terms of actually

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:02.749
implementing the lifeform into the game.

00:17:02.749 --> 00:17:07.350
While this in understandable, I do wish you
could encounter this frozen terror under the

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:09.490
ice of Below Zero...

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:14.170
And there’s one final cut lifeform we need
to cover.

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:18.800
All throughout the original game and its sequel,
mysterious structures built by a group of

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:23.440
aliens called Precursors or Architects remain
abandoned.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:29.420
The Architects only appear in game as AI or
artificial reconstructions, so we don’t

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:32.100
have any idea what they actually look like.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.520
Or do we?

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:38.590
This early concept art by Alex Ries shows
some potential designs for what the biological

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:40.820
forms of Architects might look like.

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:45.160
The Archivist: “The Architects are so mysterious
in-game, I’m curious how you decided to

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:46.510
approach their design?”

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:49.510
Alex Ries: “I wanted them to be sort of
fastidious creatures.

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:50.760
A bit precise.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:51.760
Nerdy, almost.

00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:54.360
So, I tried to design a creature along those
lines.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:17:58.030
Something that could use the tool and the
environment that we’d already seen.

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:01.750
Something that could use a tablet like computer,
but still alien.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:09.420
So, I started moving in a sort of direction
that combined amphibious references, reptilian,

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:13.450
but I think a combination of salamanders and
mantis shrimps were my main inspiration.”

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:15.770
The Archivist: “That’s so cool!”

00:18:15.770 --> 00:18:23.690
We’ve now seen an incredible variety of
lifeforms that almost swam in the oceans of

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:28.970
Planet 4546B, and learned a lot about creature
design along the way.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:36.130
A tremendous thank you to Alex Ries for sharing
insights into his design process.

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:41.000
If you enjoyed discovering the unseen creatures
of Subnautica and want to see more, you can

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.810
support Alex Ries on Patreon using the link
in the description.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.840
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:52.470
